<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Lindsey</h1><p class="st-meta">500 CE – 800 CE (approx.) · capital Lincoln</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small Anglian kingdom in north Lincolnshire centred on Roman Lincoln, briefly fought over by Northumbria and Mercia before Mercian absorption.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Lindsey took its name from the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindum-colonia">Lindum</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a>), and Anglian settlers established a kingdom there in the late 5th or 6th century. A list of its kings survives in a collection of royal genealogies, tracing them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/woden">Woden</a>, ending with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aldfrith-of-lindsey">Aldfrith</a> in the late 8th century.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The kingdom lay between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/humber">Humber</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-witham">Witham</a>, bounded by fen and sea, and was too small to stand alone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paulinus-of-york">Paulinus</a> preached at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincoln-england">Lincoln</a> around 628 under Northumbrian protection, building a stone church. A separate bishopric of Lindsey was founded in 678.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Northumbria</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Mercia</a> contested Lindsey until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-trent">Battle of the Trent</a> in 679 left it permanently Mercian. Its kings faded into dependent rulers and disappeared by about 800. The district was later settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> and formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lincolnshire">Lincolnshire</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-lindsey.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Lindsey" width="320" height="302"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lindsey.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>500 CE – 800 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lincoln</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Aldfrith</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity from the 7th century</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United Kingdom</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Lindsey, Lindissi</dd></dl></aside></div>
